WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.070
Film Courage: Mark, advice to people who are
caught in two worlds because the reality of

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:07.700
being here [Los Angeles area] is that may
be the case for a decade, for some people

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:13.331
maybe even more and at some point they will
cross over and they will be hosting their

00:00:13.331 --> 00:00:16.350
own parties and for some it may not happen.

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:21.690
Talk about keeping your mind right and keeping
your work ethic right.

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:24.830
Mark Sanderson: Well, that’s difficult.

00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:27.260
It’s like training.

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:34.600
I used to run as a runner and that discipline
of that constant was really important.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:36.629
And I would run no matter what, even if I
was tired.

00:00:36.629 --> 00:00:40.969
It’s that same work ethic that you need
for writing because I see so many writers

00:00:40.969 --> 00:00:42.039
who say “Huh?”

00:00:42.039 --> 00:00:43.370
And it’s difficult, I understand.

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:47.210
I tell some writers that “Oh, I just did
a 10-hour day.”

00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:48.210
And they are like “10 hours?”

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:53.700
And I go “I have to because I have an assignment
and it’s due and I have the luxury.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:57.469
That’s my job, thank goodness.

00:00:57.469 --> 00:01:03.010
Now when I had a day job only once in my life
for 2 years, I worked at a law firm.

00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:09.270
I had to come home from a full day of work
(I was an assistant) and at 6 o’clock make

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:11.430
dinner and then write until midnight.

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:13.469
And then go to bed, 7 a.m. go to that job.

00:01:13.469 --> 00:01:17.840
It was having 2 jobs at once and then on the
weekends people would say “Oh, come on…do

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:18.840
this and that.”

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:19.840
And I’d say “Well, I have to write.”

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:20.840
“Write?

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:21.840
What are you talking about?”

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:22.840
And I’d say “Well…”

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:27.340
And it was terribly exhausting and I can see
how it would chip away at you, especially

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:31.800
if you have a family and a lot of different
concerns pulling at you.

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:37.159
That’s why you have to really close the
door and say “This is my time.”

00:01:37.159 --> 00:01:39.149
And it cannot be interrupted.

00:01:39.149 --> 00:01:43.861
It is hard to do but you really have to stay
disciplined and also I think you have to have

00:01:43.861 --> 00:01:48.360
the fire and burning passion to really want
it more than anything else.

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:51.700
Because I never put an expiration date on
my dreams.

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:57.689
And I remember people saying…I was at dinner
one time and a friend of my brother’s said

00:01:57.689 --> 00:01:59.289
“Well, what if you don’t make it by 30?”

00:01:59.289 --> 00:02:03.430
And I was like “30?”

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:05.789
And I said “Well, I don’t know.

00:02:05.789 --> 00:02:06.789
What’s 30?”

00:02:06.789 --> 00:02:12.990
And to him he was projecting his fear upon
me and I go “This is open ended, you know?”

00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:17.930
I’m sorry because it’s good and bad because
you don’t want to fool yourself like you

00:02:17.930 --> 00:02:19.730
are saying, where after 10 years and you have
nothing.

00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:25.830
I mean I think after a certain point if you’ve
really exhausted all of your abilities and

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:31.520
nothing has happened, you might consider something…maybe
writing is not for you?

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:33.500
Maybe this pursuit of a career is not for
you?

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:37.220
And it’s a choice and no one is forcing
you to write screenplays.

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:38.420
It’s a dream.

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:40.860
It was my dream since I was 11 years old.

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:43.350
So am I going to achieve it?

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:45.650
Am I going to achieve it at a level that I
want?

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:50.980
Because when I worked in the restaurant while
I was going to film school, I knew people

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:55.310
that were actors who said “Well, it’s
not worth it for me if I’m not like Brad

00:02:55.310 --> 00:02:56.310
Pitt.”

00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:57.350
And I said “Oh, really?

00:02:57.350 --> 00:03:00.800
So you think you’re just going to fall into
it and be A-list?”

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:11.430
Now most working actors or the co-star character
actors work all their lives.

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:16.880
The leading person, the leading man, leading
woman have to carry the film and how many…I

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:23.200
mean, I’m sorry you look at it and plus
as you get older you look at it differently

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:28.341
and you say “Well, I’m blessed to get
up in the morning and do what I love for a

00:03:28.341 --> 00:03:34.270
living and get paid for it and see it end
up somewhere and people respond to it negatively

00:03:34.270 --> 00:03:35.270
or positively.”

00:03:35.270 --> 00:03:37.530
That’s it.

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:44.250
Yes, continue to strive for more and other
projects and different things but it’s hard

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:49.460
and the important thing too is to have people
around you that support your endeavor because

00:03:49.460 --> 00:03:53.900
I’ve lost friendships and relationships
with people who said “Well…”

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:55.040
And they drew a line in the sand.

00:03:55.040 --> 00:04:00.530
And they said…And I was like that’s not
a good thing to do and what about the last

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:02.080
X number of years I’ve been doing this?

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:04.730
You’re just going to have me throw that
away?

00:04:04.730 --> 00:04:09.820
You know, the lack of understanding about
how much filmmaking and writing was such a

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:14.780
part of my being almost like that of other
creative endeavors like drawing and things

00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:15.780
like that.

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:21.750
But this is like who I am and to have someone
with you not understand that it’s really…and

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:27.910
that’s the hardest part that so many elements
(just like in making a film), so many elements

00:04:27.910 --> 00:04:30.320
have to go together and click.

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:36.260
The same way with forging a career and as
you’ve interviewed many different people,

00:04:36.260 --> 00:04:41.460
everybody will tell you a different story
about how they “made it.”

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:47.620
I was doing a seminar recently and someone
asked the question about making it and I said

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:50.000
“Well, I think I don’t know if you ever
make it?”

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:55.380
I think getting your next job is making it
and continuing to stay in the game is making

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:56.380
it.

00:04:56.380 --> 00:04:57.380
There’s nothing guaranteed.

00:04:57.380 --> 00:04:59.411
If you’re on a TV show, the show is going
to end.

00:04:59.411 --> 00:05:02.040
And then you have to get up and we’re like
gypsies.

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:03.810
Find another job.

00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:06.130
I have a movie shooting right now in Los Angeles.

00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:11.910
When that ends, I mean luckily I have a few
things already going (set up) job wise, you

00:05:11.910 --> 00:05:13.280
have to find your next job.

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:16.470
It’s just the case.

00:05:16.470 --> 00:05:20.800
It’s difficult but no one said it was going
to be easy.

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:25.580
Film Courage: Before we wrap up on this topic,
I just want to ask, going back to that day

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:30.070
that you’re working this shift at wherever
it was, the top of Mulholland Drive or wherever

00:05:30.070 --> 00:05:31.070
it was…

00:05:31.070 --> 00:05:32.070
Mark Sanderson: Beverly Hills…

00:05:32.070 --> 00:05:33.070
Film Courage: Beverly Hills…okay.

00:05:33.070 --> 00:05:37.320
And let’s suppose you’ve broken down the
event and you’re driving down this mountain

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:41.200
or wherever or maybe not a mountain [in Beverly
Hills], what’s going through your mind?

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:42.440
Your name is in Variety on this day.

00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:48.760
Mark Sanderson: Well, I stopped at the newsstand
before the job and picked up a pile of Varieties,

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:49.850
you know because you have to.

00:05:49.850 --> 00:05:53.030
And reread it and I wasn’t on the cover,
the company was on the cover, but I was on

00:05:53.030 --> 00:05:56.880
like page 13 with a paragraph on how they
discovered me.

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:05.260
It’s so nice to be discovered and I’m
here pouring coffee for these people.

00:06:05.260 --> 00:06:15.150
It was freak…there was a fear that somehow
it was a big fraud and it was never going

00:06:15.150 --> 00:06:17.490
to happen even though…it was terrible.

00:06:17.490 --> 00:06:21.340
It was hard to really lock in and then you
get home and it’s very depressing.

00:06:21.340 --> 00:06:24.710
You’re like there’s no crowd cheering,
there’s no one like “Wow!”

00:06:24.710 --> 00:06:28.710
But then being pragmatic and saying “Well,
I have to pay bills.”

00:06:28.710 --> 00:06:35.490
And there’s no embarrassment about doing
a job that you have to do because that job

00:06:35.490 --> 00:06:38.900
allowed me to continue to stay in the game.

00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:44.580
Now yes, the movie was optioned and as movie
options go there’s not a lot of money sometimes

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:48.919
and then the movie developed 18 months, it
was in development with me.

00:06:48.919 --> 00:06:52.770
So you’re talking about it was 7 years after
I wrote it.

00:06:52.770 --> 00:06:57.281
So it took 7 years from when I finished it
to when I finally found the company and it

00:06:57.281 --> 00:06:58.620
was made.

00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:00.220
Not all scripts happen that way.

00:07:00.220 --> 00:07:07.440
But I’m talking about a spec and so this
is a long process with a lot of fear that

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:09.710
can be involved.

00:07:09.710 --> 00:07:16.440
I always look back on these stories but not
at that time maybe and say “This will be

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:19.419
a good story for the book someday.”

00:07:19.419 --> 00:07:22.090
Or to tell someone how these things happen.

00:07:22.090 --> 00:07:25.870
There’s a reason why I think we go through
everything is to learn something.

00:07:25.870 --> 00:07:31.699
And if we do, maybe pay it forward and that’s
kind of my reasoning for the book [A Screenwriter’s

00:07:31.699 --> 00:07:32.699
Journey to Success: Tips, tricks and tactics
to survive as a working screenwriter in Hollywood]

00:07:32.699 --> 00:07:37.760
is to try to pay it forward and saying I went
through all these crazy knocks and these pitfalls

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:39.460
and you will, too.

00:07:39.460 --> 00:07:41.610
Maybe more, maybe less.

00:07:41.610 --> 00:07:46.699
But maybe if you zig and zag, it will help
you a little easier.

00:07:46.699 --> 00:07:49.510
But it’s all difficult for all of us because
we also have to sit in front of the blank

00:07:49.510 --> 00:07:50.510
page.

00:07:50.510 --> 00:07:52.330
That’s where we are equals as screenwriters.

